"hadiths[112]{hadithnumber,text}:"
139901,Yahya related to me from Malik from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Zaynab bint Abi Salama from Umm Salama
139902,Yahya related to me from Malik from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Zaynab bint Abi Salama from Umm Salama
140001,Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Said ibn al-Musayyab that Umar ibn al-Khattab had a dispute brought to him between a muslim and a jew. Umar saw that the right belonged to the jew and decided in his favour. The jew said to him
140002,Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Said ibn al-Musayyab that Umar ibn al-Khattab had a dispute brought to him between a muslim and a jew. Umar saw that the right belonged to the jew and decided in his favour. The jew said to him
140101,Yahya related to me from Malik from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm from his father from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Uthman from Abu Amra al-Ansari from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani that the Messenger of Allah
140102,Yahya related to me from Malik from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm from his father from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Uthman from Abu Amra al-Ansari from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani that the Messenger of Allah
140201,"Yahya related to me from Malik that it reached him that Muhammad ibn Sirin used to say: ""Do not sell grain in its ears until it turns white (ripens)."" Malik said: Whoever buys food at a known price to a named term, then when the term comes due, the one who owes the food says to the owner: ""I have no food, so sell me the food you have on me on credit."" The owner says: ""This is not proper because the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade selling food until it is taken possession of."" The debtor says: ""Sell me food on credit until I pay you."" This is not proper because he only gives him food then returns it to him. The gold he gave becomes the price of what was due on him, and the food he gave becomes permissible between them. That would be selling food before taking possession if they did that. Malik said: Regarding a man who has food due from a man (he bought it from him) and his creditor has food due from a man like that food. The debtor says to his creditor: ""I refer you to a debtor of mine who has like the food you have on me, for your food which you have on me."" Malik said: If the food he owes is only food he bought, and he wants to refer his creditor with food he bought, that is not proper. That is selling food before taking possession. But if the food is a salam (advance) due immediately, there is no harm in referring with it because that is not a sale. Selling food before taking possession is not permitted due to the Prophet's ﷺ prohibition, except that the people of knowledge have agreed that there is no harm in partnership (shirkah), tawliyah (selling at cost), and iqalah (rescission) in food and other things. Malik said: That is because the people of knowledge treated it as customary practice (ma'ruf), not as sale. Like a man who advances deficient dirhams (nuqqas) and is paid full weight dirhams (wazinah) with excess. That is permitted for him. But if he bought deficient dirhams for full weight, that would not be permitted even if he stipulated full weight when he advanced it and he only gave deficient. Malik said: Similar to that is that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade muzabana (selling fresh dates on the palm for dried dates by estimation) but granted concession in 'ariyya (selling by estimation). The difference is that muzabana is sale by way of comparison and trade, while 'ariyya is by way of custom, no comparison in it. Malik said: A man should not buy food for a quarter, third, or fraction of a dirham on condition that he be given food on credit. But no harm in buying food for a fraction of a dirham on credit, then giving a dirham and taking with the remainder of his dirham some merchandise, because he gave the fraction due on him in silver and took merchandise with the rest of his dirham. No harm in that. Malik said: No harm in a man placing a dirham with a man, then taking from him for a quarter, third, or known fraction a known merchandise. If there is no known price in that and the man says: ""I take from you at the price of each day,"" that is not permitted because it is gharar (uncertainty), it decreases once and increases once, and they did not separate on a known sale. Malik said: Whoever sells food in bulk (jizaf) without excepting anything, then decides to buy some of it back, it is not proper for him to buy any of it except what he was allowed to except, which is a third or less. If it exceeds a third, it becomes muzabana and what is disliked. He should not buy any of it except what he was allowed to except. He is not allowed to except more than a third. This is the matter about which there is no difference among us."
140202,"Yahya related to me from Malik that he had heard that Muhammad Sirin used to say, ""Do not sell grain on the ears until it is white."" Malik said, ""If someone buys food for a known price to be delivered at a stated date, and when the date comes, the one who owes the food says, 'I do not have any food, sell me the food which I owe you with delayed terms.' The owner of the food says, 'This is not good, because the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, forbade selling food until the deal was completed.' The one who owes the food says to his creditor, 'Sell me any kind of food on delayed terms until I discharge the debt to you.' This is not good because he gives him food and then he returns it to him. The gold which he gave him becomes the price of that which is his right against him and the food which he gave him becomes what clears what is between them. If they do that, it becomes the sale of food before the deal is complete."" Malik spoke about a man who was owed food which he had purchased from a man and this man was owed the like of that food by another man. The one who owed the food said to his creditor, ""I will refer you to my debtor who owes me the same amount of food as I owe you, so that you may obtain the food which I owe you ."" Malik said, ""If the man who had to deliver the food, had gone out, and bought the food to pay off his creditor, that is not good. That is selling food before taking possession of it. If the food is an advance which falls due at that particular time, there is no harm in paying off his creditor with it because that is nota sale. It is not halal to sell food before receiving it in full since the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, forbade that. However, the people of knowledge agree that there is no harm in partnership, transfer of responsibility and revocation in sales of food and other goods."" Malik said, ""That is because the people of knowledge consider it as a favour rendered. They do not consider it as a sale. It is like a man lending light dirhams. He is then paid back in dirhams of full weight, and so gets back more than he lent. That is halal for him and permitted. Had a man bought defective dirhams from him as being the full weight, that would not be halal. Had it been stipulated to him that he lend full weight in dirhams, and then he gave faulty ones, that would not be halal for him."""""""
140301,"Yahya related to me from Malik, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Hanzalah."
"140302","Yahya related to me from Malik, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Hanzalah ibn Qays al-Zurayqi, from Rafi' ibn Khadij, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, forbade the renting of farmland. Hanzalah said: ""So I asked Rafi' ibn Khadij, 'What about with gold and silver?' He said: 'As for with gold and silver, there is no harm in it.'"""
140401,Yahya said from Malik that he heard from Sulayman ibn Yasar and others that when they were asked whether the testimony of a man flogged for a hadd crime was permitted
140402,Yahya said from Malik that he heard from Sulayman ibn Yasar and others that when they were asked whether the testimony of a man flogged for a hadd crime was permitted
140501,"And Yahya narrated to me, from Malik, from Yunus bin Yusuf, from Sa'id bin Al-Musayyab, that Umar bin Al-Khattab passed by Hatib bin Abi Balta'ah while he was selling his raisins in the market. Umar bin Al-Khattab said to him: \Either you increase the price"
140502,"And Malik narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, that he asked Salim bin Abdullah bin Umar about renting out farms. He said: \There is no harm in it for gold and silver.\"" Ibn Shihab said: \""So I said to him: What do you think about the hadith that is mentioned from Rafi' bin Khadij?\"" He said: \""Rafi' exaggerated. If I had a farm"
140601,Yahya related to me from Malik that it reached him that Uthman ibn Affan used to forbid hoarding (al-hukra).
140602,"Malik related to me that it reached him that Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf rented a piece of land, and it remained in his possession for rent until he died. His son said: ""I only thought it was ours because of how long it had remained in his possession, until he mentioned it to us at his death, and he ordered us to pay off something that was due on him from its rent, in gold or silver."""""""
140701,From Malik from Abu'z-Zinad that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd ar-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn al-Khattab who was the governor of Kufa
140702,From Malik from Abu'z-Zinad that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd ar-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn al-Khattab who was the governor of Kufa
140801,"Malik related to me that he heard that Abu Salama ibn Abd ar- Rahman and Sulayman ibn Yasar were both asked, ""Does one pronounce judgement on the basis of an oath with one witness?"" They both said, ""Yes."" Malik said, ""The precedent of the sunna in judging by an oath with one witness is that if the plaintiff takes an oath with his witness, he is confirmed in his right. If he draws back and refuses to take an oath, the defendant is made to take an oath. If he takes an oath, the claim against him is dropped. If he refuses to take an oath, the claim is confirmed against him."" Malik said, ""This procedure pertains to property cases in particular. It does not occur in any of the hadd-punishments, nor in marriage, divorce, freeing slaves, theft or slander. If some one says, 'Freeing slaves comes under property,' he has erred. It is not as he said. Had it been as he said, a slave could take an oath with one witness, if he could find one, that his master had freed him. ""However, when a slave lays claim to a piece of property, he can take an oath with one witness and demand his right as the freeman demands his right."" Malik said, ""The sunna with us is that when a slave brings somebody who witnesses that he has been set free, his master is made to take an oath that he has not freed him, and the slave's claim is dropped."" Malik said, ""The sunna about divorce is also like that with us. When a woman brings somebody who witnesses that her husband has divorced her, the husband is made to take an oath that he has not divorced her. If he takes the oath, the divorce does not proceed . "" Malik said, ""There is only one sunna of bringing a witness in cases of divorce and freeing a slave. The right to make an oath only belongs to the husband of the woman, and the master of the slave. Freeing is a hadd matter, and the testimony of women is not permitted in it because when a slave is freed, his inviolability is affirmed and the hadd punishments are applied for and against him. If he commits fornication and he is a muhsan, he is stoned. If he kills a slave, he is killed for it. Inheritance is established for him, between him and whoever inherits from him. If somebody disputes this, arguing that if a man frees his slave and then a man comes to demand from the master of the slave payment of a debt, and a man and two women testify to his right, that establishes the right against the master of the slave so that his freeing him is cancelled if he only has the slave as property, inferring by this case that the testimony of women is permitted in cases of setting free. The case is not as he suggests (i.e. it is a case of property not freeing). It is like a man who frees his slave, and then the claimant of a debt comes to the master and takes an oath with one witness, demanding his right. By that, the freeing of the slave would be cancelled. Or else a man comes who has frequent dealings and transactions with the master of the slave. He claims that he is owed money by the master of the slave. Someone says to the master of the slave, 'Take an oath that you don't owe what he claims'. If he draws back and refuses to take an oath, the one making the claim takes an oath and his right against the master of the slave is confirmed. That would cancel the freeing of the slave if it is confirmed that property is owed by the master."" Malik said, ""It is the same case with a man who marries a slave-girl and then the master of the slave-girl comes to the man who has married her and claims, 'You and so-and-so have bought my slave-girl from me for such an amount of dinars. The husband of the slave-girl denies that. The master of the slave-girl brings a man and two women and they testify to what he has said. The sale is confirmed and his claim is considered true. So the slave-girl is haram for her husband and they have to separate, even though the testimony of women is not accepted in divorce."" Malik said, ""It is also the same case with a man who accuses a free man, so the hadd falls on him. A man and two women come and testify that the one accused is a slave. That would remove the hadd from the accused after it had befallen him, even though the testimony of women is not accepted in accusations involving hadd punishments."" Malik said, ""Another similar case in which judgement appears to go against the precedent of the sunna is that two women testify that a child is born alive and so it is necessary for him to inherit if a situation arises where he is entitled to inherit, and the child's property goes to those who inherit from him, if he dies, and it is not necessary that the two women witnesses should be accompanied by a man or an oath even though it may involve vast properties of gold, silver, live-stock, gardens and slaves and other properties. However, had two women testified to one dirham or more or less than that in a property case, their testimony would not affect anything and would not be permitted unless there was a witness or an oath with them."" Malik said, ""There are people who say that an oath is not acceptable with only one witness and they argue by the word of Allah the Blessed, the Exalted, and His word is the Truth, 'And call in to witness two witnesses, men; or if the two be not men, then one man and two women, such witnesses as you approve of.' (Sura 2 ayat 282). Such people argue that if he does not bring one man and two women, he has no claim and he is not allowed to take an oath with one witness."" Malik said, ""Part of the proof against those who argue this, is to reply to them, 'Do you think that if a man claimed property from a man, the one claimed from would not swear that the claim was false?' If he swears, the claim against him is dropped. If he refuses to take an oath, the claimant is made to take an oath that his claim is true, and his right against his companion is established. There is no dispute about this with any of the people nor in any country. By what does he take this? In what place in the Book of Allah does he find it? So if he confirms this, let him confirm the oath with one witness, even if it is not in the Book of Allah, the Mighty, the Majestic! It is enough that this is the precedent of the sunna. However, man wants to recognise the proper course of action and the location of the proof. In this there is a clarification for what is obscure about that, if Allah ta'ala wills"
140802,"Malik related to me that he heard that Abu Salama ibn Abd ar- Rahman and Sulayman ibn Yasar were both asked, ""Does one pronounce judgement on the basis of an oath with one witness?"" They both said, ""Yes."" Malik said, ""The precedent of the sunna in judging by an oath with one witness is that if the plaintiff takes an oath with his witness, he is confirmed in his right. If he draws back and refuses to take an oath, the defendant is made to take an oath. If he takes an oath, the claim against him is dropped. If he refuses to take an oath, the claim is confirmed against him."" Malik said, ""This procedure pertains to property cases in particular. It does not occur in any of the hadd-punishments, nor in marriage, divorce, freeing slaves, theft or slander. If some one says, 'Freeing slaves comes under property,' he has erred. It is not as he said. Had it been as he said, a slave could take an oath with one witness, if he could find one, that his master had freed him. ""However, when a slave lays claim to a piece of property, he can take an oath with one witness and demand his right as the freeman demands his right."" Malik said, ""The sunna with us is that when a slave brings somebody who witnesses that he has been set free, his master is made to take an oath that he has not freed him, and the slave's claim is dropped."" Malik said, ""The sunna about divorce is also like that with us. When a woman brings somebody who witnesses that her husband has divorced her, the husband is made to take an oath that he has not divorced her. If he takes the oath, the divorce does not proceed . "" Malik said, ""There is only one sunna of bringing a witness in cases of divorce and freeing a slave. The right to make an oath only belongs to the husband of the woman, and the master of the slave. Freeing is a hadd matter, and the testimony of women is not permitted in it because when a slave is freed, his inviolability is affirmed and the hadd punishments are applied for and against him. If he commits fornication and he is a muhsan, he is stoned. If he kills a slave, he is killed for it. Inheritance is established for him, between him and whoever inherits from him. If somebody disputes this, arguing that if a man frees his slave and then a man comes to demand from the master of the slave payment of a debt, and a man and two women testify to his right, that establishes the right against the master of the slave so that his freeing him is cancelled if he only has the slave as property, inferring by this case that the testimony of women is permitted in cases of setting free. The case is not as he suggests (i.e. it is a case of property not freeing). It is like a man who frees his slave, and then the claimant of a debt comes to the master and takes an oath with one witness, demanding his right. By that, the freeing of the slave would be cancelled. Or else a man comes who has frequent dealings and transactions with the master of the slave. He claims that he is owed money by the master of the slave. Someone says to the master of the slave, 'Take an oath that you don't owe what he claims'. If he draws back and refuses to take an oath, the one making the claim takes an oath and his right against the master of the slave is confirmed. That would cancel the freeing of the slave if it is confirmed that property is owed by the master."" Malik said, ""It is the same case with a man who marries a slave-girl and then the master of the slave-girl comes to the man who has married her and claims, 'You and so-and-so have bought my slave-girl from me for such an amount of dinars. The husband of the slave-girl denies that. The master of the slave-girl brings a man and two women and they testify to what he has said. The sale is confirmed and his claim is considered true. So the slave-girl is haram for her husband and they have to separate, even though the testimony of women is not accepted in divorce."" Malik said, ""It is also the same case with a man who accuses a free man, so the hadd falls on him. A man and two women come and testify that the one accused is a slave. That would remove the hadd from the accused after it had befallen him, even though the testimony of women is not accepted in accusations involving hadd punishments."" Malik said, ""Another similar case in which judgement appears to go against the precedent of the sunna is that two women testify that a child is born alive and so it is necessary for him to inherit if a situation arises where he is entitled to inherit, and the child's property goes to those who inherit from him, if he dies, and it is not necessary that the two women witnesses should be accompanied by a man or an oath even though it may involve vast properties of gold, silver, live-stock, gardens and slaves and other properties. However, had two women testified to one dirham or more or less than that in a property case, their testimony would not affect anything and would not be permitted unless there was a witness or an oath with them."" Malik said, ""There are people who say that an oath is not acceptable with only one witness and they argue by the word of Allah the Blessed, the Exalted, and His word is the Truth, 'And call in to witness two witnesses, men; or if the two be not men, then one man and two women, such witnesses as you approve of.' (Sura 2 ayat 282). Such people argue that if he does not bring one man and two women, he has no claim and he is not allowed to take an oath with one witness."" Malik said, ""Part of the proof against those who argue this, is to reply to them, 'Do you think that if a man claimed property from a man, the one claimed from would not swear that the claim was false?' If he swears, the claim against him is dropped. If he refuses to take an oath, the claimant is made to take an oath that his claim is true, and his right against his companion is established. There is no dispute about this with any of the people nor in any country. By what does he take this? In what place in the Book of Allah does he find it? So if he confirms this, let him confirm the oath with one witness, even if it is not in the Book of Allah, the Mighty, the Majestic! It is enough that this is the precedent of the sunna. However, man wants to recognise the proper course of action and the location of the proof. In this there is a clarification for what is obscure about that, if Allah ta'ala wills"
140901,Yahya said that Malik spoke about a man who died and had a debt owing to him and there was one witness
140902,Yahya said that Malik spoke about a man who died and had a debt owing to him and there was one witness
141001,"Yahya related to me from Malik from Nafi' from Abdullah ibn Umar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the sale of habal al-habala. It was a sale practiced by the people of the Jahiliyya where a man would buy the offspring of a she-camel until she gives birth, then the offspring of that which is in her womb."
141002,Malik related to me that it reached him from Sulayman ibn Yasar the like of that.
141101,"Yahya related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab that he said: ""There is no riba in animals. Rather, only three things were forbidden in animals: al-madamin, al-malaqih, and habal al-habala. Al-madamin is the sale of what is in the wombs of female camels. Al-malaqih is the sale of what is on the backs of male camels (i.e., their future offspring)."" Malik said: ""No one should buy a specific animal when it is absent from him, even if he has seen it and approved of it, on condition that he pays its price, whether near or far."" Malik said: ""That was only disliked because the seller benefits from the price, and it is not known whether that merchandise will be found as the buyer saw it or not. For that reason it was disliked. There is no harm in it if it is guaranteed and described."""""""
141102,"Yahya said: Malik said from Muhammad ibn Umara from Abu Bakr ibn Hazm that Uthman ibn Affan said: ""When boundaries are established in land, there is no right of preemption (shuf'a) in it, nor in a well, nor in a palm pollinator."""""""
141201,Malik related to me from al-Ala ibn Abd ar-Rahman from Mabad ibn Kab as-Salami from his brother Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik al-Ansari from Abu Umama that the Messenger of Allah
141202,Malik related to me from al-Ala ibn Abd ar-Rahman from Mabad ibn Kab as-Salami from his brother Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik al-Ansari from Abu Umama that the Messenger of Allah
141301,Yahya said that Malik had said from Da'ud ibn al-Husayn that he heard Abu Ghatafan ibn Tarif al-Muriyi say
141302,Yahya said that Malik had said from Da'ud ibn al-Husayn that he heard Abu Ghatafan ibn Tarif al-Muriyi say
141401,"Yahya related to me from Malik from Abu al-Zinad from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab that he used to say: ""The sale of a live animal for meat (slaughtered) was forbidden."" Abu al-Zinad said: I said to Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab: ""What do you think of a man who buys a she-camel for ten sheep?"" Sa'id said: ""If he bought it to slaughter it, there is no good in that."" Abu al-Zinad said: ""All the people I encountered forbade the sale of a live animal for meat."" Abu al-Zinad said: ""That used to be written in the contracts of the governors in the time of Aban ibn Uthman and Hisham ibn Isma'il, forbidding that."""""""
141402,"Yahya related to us from Malik from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm from his father from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Uthman from Abu Amra al-Ansari from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Shall I not inform you of the best of witnesses? The one who brings his testimony before he is asked for it, or informs of his testimony before he is asked for it."""""""
141501,"Malik related to me from Rabi'a ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman that he said: ""There came to Umar ibn al-Khattab a man from the people of Iraq..."""""""
141502,"Malik related to me from Rabi'a ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman that he said: ""There came to Umar ibn al-Khattab a man from the people of Iraq who said: 'I have come to you for a matter that has no head nor tail.' Umar said: 'What is it?' He said: 'False testimony has appeared in our land.' Umar said: 'Has that happened?' He said: 'Yes.' Umar said: 'By Allah, no man will be given ease in Islam except through the upright (al-udul).'"""""""
141601,"And Yahya narrated to me, from Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Bakr bin Abdur Rahman bin Al-Harith bin Hisham, from Abu Mas'ud Al-Ansari, that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) prohibited the price of a dog, the dowry of a prostitute, and the fee of a soothsayer. Meaning by the dowry of a prostitute what a woman is given for fornication, and the fee of a soothsayer is his bribe and what is given for soothsaying."
141602,"And Malik narrated to me that it reached him that Umar bin Al-Khattab said: \The testimony of an opponent or someone suspected is not permissible.\"""""""""
141701,"Malik related to me from Abd ar-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Qari that his father said, ""A man came to Umar ibn al- Khattab from Abu Musa al-Ashari. Umar asked after various people, and he informed him. Then Umar inquired, 'Do you have any recent news?' He said, 'Yes. A man has become a kafir after his Islam.' Umar asked, 'What have you done with him?' He said, 'We let him approach and struck off his head.' Umar said, 'Didn't you imprison him for three days and feed him a loaf of bread every day and call on him to tawba that he might turn in tawba and return to the command of Allah?' Then Umar said, 'O Allah! I was not present and I did not order it and I am not pleased since it has come to me"
141702,"Malik related to me from Abd ar-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Qari that his father said, ""A man came to Umar ibn al- Khattab from Abu Musa al-Ashari. Umar asked after various people, and he informed him. Then Umar inquired, 'Do you have any recent news?' He said, 'Yes. A man has become a kafir after his Islam.' Umar asked, 'What have you done with him?' He said, 'We let him approach and struck off his head.' Umar said, 'Didn't you imprison him for three days and feed him a loaf of bread every day and call on him to tawba that he might turn in tawba and return to the command of Allah?' Then Umar said, 'O Allah! I was not present and I did not order it and I am not pleased since it has come to me"
141801,"Yahya related to me from Malik from Yahya ibn Sa'id from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad that he said: ""I heard Abdullah ibn Abbas, and a man asked him about a man who gave an advance payment (salam) for slaves (saba'ib) and then wanted to sell them before taking possession of them. Ibn Abbas said: 'That is silver for silver,' and he disliked that."" Malik said: ""And that, in our view - and Allah knows best - is because he wanted to sell them back to the one he bought them from for more than the price he paid in the advance. But if he sold them to someone other than the one he bought them from, there would be no harm in it."" Malik said: ""The agreed-upon practice among us concerning one who gives an advance payment for slaves, livestock, or goods: if each item is described and an advance is made to a fixed term, and the term comes due, the buyer may not sell any of that back to the one he bought it from for more than the advance price before taking possession of what he advanced for. That is because if he does so, it is usury (riba): the buyer, if he gave the seller dinars or dirhams and benefited from them, then when the goods became due and the buyer had not taken possession, he sold them back to the seller for more than the advance price, so it became as if he returned the advance to him and added to it from his own wealth."" Malik said: ""Whoever advances gold or silver for livestock or goods, described, to a named term, then when the term comes due, there is no harm in the buyer selling those goods back to the seller before the term falls due or after it falls due, for other goods (arad) which he takes immediately and does not defer, whatever the amount of those goods, except food. For it is not lawful to sell it until he takes possession of it. And the buyer may sell those goods to someone other than the one he bought them from for gold, silver, or other goods which he takes immediately and does not defer. Because if he defers that, it becomes reprehensible and enters into what is disliked of selling a debt for a debt (kali' bi al-kali'). And selling a debt for a debt is when a man sells a debt he has on a man for a debt on another man."" Malik said: ""And whoever advances for goods to a term, and those goods are of what is not eaten or drunk, the buyer may sell them to whomever he wishes for cash or goods before he receives them in full, provided it is not to the one he bought them from. And he should not sell them to the one he bought them from except for goods which he takes immediately and does not defer."" Malik said: ""And if the goods have not yet fallen due, there is no harm in selling them to the seller for goods different from them, a clear difference, which he takes immediately and does not defer."" Malik said: ""Concerning one who advances dinars or dirhams for four described garments to a term: when the term falls due, the owner demands them but does not find them with him, and finds instead garments inferior to them of the same type. The one who owes the garments says: 'I will give you eight garments of these mine in their place.' There is no harm in that if he takes those garments he is giving before they part. But if a term enters into it, it is not valid. And even if it is before the term falls due, it is not valid either, unless he sells him garments not of the type of the garments he advanced for."""""""
141802,Yahya related to me from Malik from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih as- Samman from his father from Abu Hurayra that Sad ibn Ubada said to the Messenger of Allah
141901,Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Said ibn al-Musayyab that a Syrian man called Ibn Khaybari found a man with his wife and killed him
141902,Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Said ibn al-Musayyab that a Syrian man called Ibn Khaybari found a man with his wife and killed him
142001,Yahya said that Malik related from Ibn Shihab that Sunayn Abi Jamila
142002,Yahya said that Malik related from Ibn Shihab that Sunayn Abi Jamila
142101,Yahya said from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Urwa ibn az-Zubayr that A'isha
142102,Yahya said from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Urwa ibn az-Zubayr that A'isha
142201,Malik related to me from Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Hadi from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith at-Taymi from Sulayman ibn Yasar from Abdullah ibn Abi Umayya that a woman's husband died
142202,Malik related to me from Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Hadi from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith at-Taymi from Sulayman ibn Yasar from Abdullah ibn Abi Umayya that a woman's husband died
142301,"Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Sulayman ibn Yasar that Umar ibn al-Khattab used to attach the children of the Jahiliyya to whoever claimed them in Islam. Two men came and each of them claimed a woman's child. Umar ibn al-Khattab summoned a person who scrutinized features and he looked at them. The scrutinizer said, ""They both share in him."" Umar ibn al-Khattab hit him with a whip. Then he summoned the woman, and said, ""Tell me your tale."" She said, ""It was this one (indicating one of the two men) who used to come to me while I was with my people's camels. He did not leave me until he thought and I thought that I was pregnant. Then he left me, and blood flowed from me, and this other one took his place. I do not know from which of them the child is."" The scrutinizer said, ""Allah is greater."" Umar said to the child, ""Go to whichever of them you wish"
142302,"Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Sulayman ibn Yasar that Umar ibn al-Khattab used to attach the children of the Jahiliyya to whoever claimed them in Islam. Two men came and each of them claimed a woman's child. Umar ibn al-Khattab summoned a person who scrutinized features and he looked at them. The scrutinizer said, ""They both share in him."" Umar ibn al-Khattab hit him with a whip. Then he summoned the woman, and said, ""Tell me your tale."" She said, ""It was this one (indicating one of the two men) who used to come to me while I was with my people's camels. He did not leave me until he thought and I thought that I was pregnant. Then he left me, and blood flowed from me, and this other one took his place. I do not know from which of them the child is."" The scrutinizer said, ""Allah is greater."" Umar said to the child, ""Go to whichever of them you wish"
142401,Malik related to me that he had heard that Umar ibn al-Khattab or Uthman ibn Affan gave a judgement about a slave woman who misled a man about herself and said that she was free. He married her and she bore children. It was decided that he should ransom his children with their like of slaves. Yahya said that he heard Malik say
142402,Malik related to me that he had heard that Umar ibn al-Khattab or Uthman ibn Affan gave a judgement about a slave woman who misled a man about herself and said that she was free. He married her and she bore children. It was decided that he should ransom his children with their like of slaves. Yahya said that he heard Malik say
142501,"Malik narrated to me, from Al-Ala' ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ma'bad ibn Ka'b al-Silmi..."
"142502","Malik narrated to me, from Al-Ala' ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ma'bad ibn Ka'b al-Silmi, from his brother, Abdullah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik al-Ansari, from Abu Umamah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever seizes the right of a Muslim by his oath, Allah has forbidden Paradise for him and made the Fire obligatory for him."" They said: ""Even if it is something small, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Even if it is a twig of Arak, even if it is a twig of Arak, even if it is a twig of Arak."" He said this three times."
142601,"Malik related to me from Nafi that Safiyya bint Abi Ubayd informed him that Umar ibn al-Khattab said, ""What is the matter with men who have intercourse with their slave-girls and then leave them to go? No slave-girl comes to me whose master confesses that he has had intercourse with her but that I connect her child to him, whether or not he has practised coitus interruptus or left off from intercourse with her."" Yahya said that he heard Malik say, ""What is done in our community about an umm walad who commits a crime is that her master is liable for what she has done up to her value. He does not have to surrender her, and he cannot be made to bear more than her value for her crime"
142602,"Malik related to me from Nafi that Safiyya bint Abi Ubayd informed him that Umar ibn al-Khattab said, ""What is the matter with men who have intercourse with their slave-girls and then leave them to go? No slave-girl comes to me whose master confesses that he has had intercourse with her but that I connect her child to him, whether or not he has practised coitus interruptus or left off from intercourse with her."" Yahya said that he heard Malik say, ""What is done in our community about an umm walad who commits a crime is that her master is liable for what she has done up to her value. He does not have to surrender her, and he cannot be made to bear more than her value for her crime"
142701,Yahya related from Malik from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father that the Messenger of Allah
142702,Yahya related from Malik from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father that the Messenger of Allah
142801,"Malik related to me from Ibn Shihab from Salim ibn Abdullah from his father that Umar ibn al-Khattab said, ""Whoever revives dead land, it belongs to him."" Malik said, ""That is what is done in our community"
142802,"Malik related to me from Ibn Shihab from Salim ibn Abdullah from his father that Umar ibn al-Khattab said, ""Whoever revives dead land, it belongs to him."" Malik said, ""That is what is done in our community"
142901,Yahya related to me from Malik from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm that he heard that the Messenger of Allah
142902,Yahya related to me from Malik from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm that he heard that the Messenger of Allah
143001,Malik related to me from Abu'z-Zinad from al-Araj from Abu Hurayra that the Messenger of Allah
143002,Malik related to me from Abu'z-Zinad from al-Araj from Abu Hurayra that the Messenger of Allah
143101,Malik related to me from Abu'r-Rijal Muhammad ibn Abd ar-Rahman from his mother Amra bint Abd ar-Rahman that she informed him that the Messenger of Allah
143102,Malik related to me from Abu'r-Rijal Muhammad ibn Abd ar-Rahman from his mother Amra bint Abd ar-Rahman that she informed him that the Messenger of Allah
143201,Yahya related to me from Malik from Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini from his father that the Messenger of Allah
143202,Yahya related to me from Malik from Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini from his father that the Messenger of Allah
143301,"And Malik related to me from Musa ibn Maysara that he heard a man ask Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab: ""I am a man who sells on credit (bi al-dayn)."" Sa'id said: ""Do not sell except what you have brought to your dwelling."" Malik said: ""Concerning the one who buys goods from a man on condition that he delivers those goods to a named term - either for a market he hopes will be favorable, or for a need in that time he stipulated - then the seller fails to deliver at that term, and the buyer wants to return those goods to the seller: that is not for the buyer, and the sale is binding on him. And if the seller brings those goods before the term falls due, the buyer is not compelled to take them."" Malik said: ""Concerning the one who buys food, measures it, then someone comes to buy it from him and informs the comer that he has measured it for himself and taken full possession. The buyer wants to believe him and take it by his measure: whatever is sold on this description for cash, there is no harm in it. But whatever is sold on this description to a term, it is disliked until the other buyer measures it for himself. And the term sale is disliked only because it is a means to usury and a fear that it might be conducted on this basis without measure or weight. If it is to a term, it is disliked, and there is no difference of opinion on this among us."" Malik said: ""One should not buy a debt on an absent man, nor a present one, except with an acknowledgment from the one on whom the debt is, nor on a dead man, even if the estate of the dead man is known. That is because buying that is uncertainty (gharar); it is not known whether it will be fulfilled or not."" He said: ""The explanation of what is disliked of that is that if one buys a debt on an absent or dead man, it is not known what debts may attach to the dead man that were not known. If a debt attaches to the dead man, the price the buyer paid goes to waste."" Malik said: ""And in that there is another defect: he bought something not guaranteed to him, and if it is not fulfilled, his price goes to waste. So this is gharar which is not valid."" Malik said: ""The distinction between 'a man should not sell except what he has' and 'a man may advance for something not in his possession' is that the practitioner of 'ina (a usurious loan disguised as a sale) only brings the gold he wants to buy with and says: 'These are ten dinars; what do you want me to buy for you with them?' As if he sells ten dinars cash for fifteen dinars to a term. Therefore this was disliked. And that is the deception and the fraud."""""""
143302,Malik related to me from Ibn Shihab from al-Araj from Abu Hurayra that the Messenger of Allah
143401,Malik related to me from Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini from his father that ad-Dahhak ibn Khalifa watered his irrigation ditch from a large source of water. He wanted to have it pass through the land of Muhammad ibn Maslama
143402,Malik related to me from Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini from his father that ad-Dahhak ibn Khalifa watered his irrigation ditch from a large source of water. He wanted to have it pass through the land of Muhammad ibn Maslama
143501,"Malik related to me from Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini that his father said, ""I was sitting with Abdullah ibn Zayd ibn Asim al-Mazini, the grandfather of Amr ibn Yahya, and Abbad ibn Tamim asked him about the wudu of the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace. Abdullah ibn Zayd called for a vessel"
143502,"Yahya related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Abu Bakr ibn Abd ar-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham that the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said, ""Whenever a man sells wares and then the buyer becomes bankrupt and the seller has not taken any of the price and he finds some of his property intact with the buyer, he is more entitled to it than anyone else. If the buyer dies, then the seller is the same as other creditors with respect to it."""""""
143601,"Yahya related to me from Malik that Thawr ibn Zayd ad-Dili said, ""It reached me that when the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, spoke a word, he repeated it three times so that it would be understood, and when he came to a people he greeted them three times."""
143602,"Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm from Umar ibn Abdal-Aziz from Abu Bakr ibn Abd ar-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham from Abu Hurayra that the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said, ""If anyone goes bankrupt, and a man finds his own property intact with him, he is more entitled to it than anyone else."" Malik spoke about a man who sold a man wares, and the buyer went bankrupt. He said, ""The seller takes whatever of his goods he finds. If the buyer has sold some of them and distributed them, the seller of the wares is more entitled to them than the creditors. What the buyer has distributed does not prevent the seller from taking whatever of it he finds. It is the seller's right if he has received any of the price from the buyer and he wants to return it to take what he finds of his wares, and in what he does not find, he is like the creditors."" Malik spoke about some one who bought spun wool or a plot of land, and then did some work on it, like building a house on the plot of land or weaving the spun wool into cloth. Then he went bankrupt after he had bought it, and the original owner of the plot said, ""I will take the plot and whatever structure is on it."" Malik said, ""That structure is not his. However, the plot and what is in it that the buyer has improved is appraised. Then one sees what the price of the plot is and how much of that value is the price of the structure. They are partners in that. The owner of the plot has as much as his portion, and the creditors have the amount of the portion of the structure."" Malik said, ""The explanation of that is that the value of it all is fifteen hundred dirhams. The value of the plot is five hundred dirhams, and the value of the building is one thousand dirhams. The owner of the plot has a third, and the creditors have two-thirds."" Malik said, ""It is like that with spinning and other things of the same nature in these circumstances and the buyer has a debt which he cannot pay. This is the behaviour in such cases."" Malik said, ""As for goods which have been sold and which the buyer does not improve, but those goods sell well and have gone up in price, so their owner wants them and the creditors also want to seize them, then the creditors choose between giving the owner of the goods the price for which he sold them and not giving him any loss and surrendering his goods to him. ""If the price of the goods has gone down, the one who sold them has a choice. If he likes, he can take his goods and he has no claim to any of his debtor's property, and that is his right. If he likes, he can be one of the creditors and take a portion of his due and not take his goods. That is up to him."" Malik said about someone who bought a slave-girl or animal and she gave birth in his possession and the buyer went bankrupt, ""The slave-girl or the animal and the offspring belong to the seller unless the creditors desire it. In that case they give him his complete due and they take it."""""""
143701,"Yahya said that he heard Malik speak about a man who died and left properties in Aliya and Safila (outlying districts of Madina). He said, ""Unirrigated naturally watered land is not in the same category as irrigated land unless the family are satisfied with that. Unirrigated land is only in the same category as land with a spring when it resembles it. When the properties are in one land, and are close together, each individual property is evaluated and then divided between the heirs. Dwellings and houses are in the same position."" Yahya related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Haram ibn Sad ibn Muhayyisa that a female camel of al-Bara ibn Azib entered the garden of a man and it did some damage to it. The Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, gave a judgement that the people of the garden were responsible for guarding it in the day, and the owner of the animals was liable for what the animals destroyed at night"
143702,"Yahya said that he heard Malik speak about a man who died and left properties in Aliya and Safila (outlying districts of Madina). He said, ""Unirrigated naturally watered land is not in the same category as irrigated land unless the family are satisfied with that. Unirrigated land is only in the same category as land with a spring when it resembles it. When the properties are in one land, and are close together, each individual property is evaluated and then divided between the heirs. Dwellings and houses are in the same position."" Yahya related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Haram ibn Sad ibn Muhayyisa that a female camel of al-Bara ibn Azib entered the garden of a man and it did some damage to it. The Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, gave a judgement that the people of the garden were responsible for guarding it in the day, and the owner of the animals was liable for what the animals destroyed at night"
143801,Malik related to me from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Yahya ibn Abd ar-Rahman ibn Hatib that some slaves of Hatib stole a she-camel belonging to a man from the Muzayna tribe and they slaughtered it. The case was brought before Umar ibn al-Khattab
143802,Malik related to me from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Yahya ibn Abd ar-Rahman ibn Hatib that some slaves of Hatib stole a she-camel belonging to a man from the Muzayna tribe and they slaughtered it. The case was brought before Umar ibn al-Khattab
143901,"Yahya narrated to me, from Malik, that it reached him that Umar ibn al-Khattab said regarding a man who advanced food to another man on the condition that he would deliver it in another city, that Umar ibn al-Khattab disliked this and said: \Then where is the carrying?\"" meaning its carriage."""""""
"143902","Malik narrated to me, from Nafi', from Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd, that she informed him that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: ""What is the matter with men who have intercourse with their slave women and then let them go out? No slave woman comes to me whose master admits to having had intercourse with her, except that I have joined her child to him. So send them away afterwards, or keep them."""
144001,"A free loan which you lend by which you desire the pleasure of Allah, and so you have the pleasure of Allah. A free loan which you lend by which you desire the pleasure of your companion, so you have the pleasure of your companion, and a free loan which you lend by which you take what is impure by what is pure, and that is usury."" He said, ""What do you order me to do, Abu Abd ar-Rahman?"" He said, ""I think that you should tear up the agreement. If he gives you the like of what you lent him, accept it. If he gives you less than what you lent him, take it and you will be rewarded. If he gives you better than what you lent him, of his own good will, that is his gratitude to you and you have the wage of the period you gave him the loan."""""""
144002,Malik related to me from Ibn Shihab from Urwa ibn az-Zubayr that A'isha
144101,"Malik related to me from Ibn Shihab from Urwa ibn az-Zubayr from Abd ar-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari that Umar ibn al-Khattab said, ""What is wrong with men who give their sons gifts and then keep them and if the son dies, they say, 'My property is in my possession and I did not give it to anyone.' But if they themselves are dying, they say, 'It belongs to my son, I gave it to him.' Whoever gives a gift, and does not hand it over to the one to whom it was given, the gift is invalid, and if he dies it belongs to the heirs in general"
144102,"Malik related to me from Ibn Shihab from Urwa ibn az-Zubayr from Abd ar-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari that Umar ibn al-Khattab said, ""What is wrong with men who give their sons gifts and then keep them and if the son dies, they say, 'My property is in my possession and I did not give it to anyone.' But if they themselves are dying, they say, 'It belongs to my son, I gave it to him.' Whoever gives a gift, and does not hand it over to the one to whom it was given, the gift is invalid, and if he dies it belongs to the heirs in general"
144201,"And Malik related to me that it reached him that Abdullah ibn Mas'ud used to say: ""Whoever gives an advance (aslaf salafan) should not stipulate better than it, even if it is a handful of fodder, for that is usury."" Malik said: ""The agreed-upon practice among us is that whoever borrows something of livestock with a known description and quality, there is no harm in that, and he must return its like, except for female slaves (wala'id). For in that there is feared the means to making lawful what is not lawful, so it is not valid. The explanation of what is disliked of that is that a man borrows a slave girl, has his way with her, then returns her to her owner as she is. That is not valid nor lawful, and the people of knowledge have never ceased to forbid it and not permit it for anyone."""""""
144202,"Yahya narrated to me, from Malik, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, that it reached him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning the floodwaters of Mahzoor and Muzaynab: \It is held back until the ankles"
144301,"Yahya narrated to me, from Malik, from Nafi, from Abdullah ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Let not one of you sell upon the sale of another.\"""""""""
144302,"Yahya narrated to me, from Malik, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, that it reached him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \Excess water shall not be withheld to prevent fodder.\"""""""""
144401,"Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said that Abd ar-Rahman ibn al-Qasim ibn Muhammad heard Makhul ad-Dimashqi ask al-Qasim ibn Muhammad about the life pension and what people said about it. Al- Qasim ibn Muhammad said, ""I have only come upon people who keep to the conditions they make about their property and what they are given."" Yahya said that he heard Malik say, ""What is done in our community is that the life pension reverts to the one who makes it a life pension unless he says, 'It belongs to you and your posterity"
144402,"Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said that Abd ar-Rahman ibn al-Qasim ibn Muhammad heard Makhul ad-Dimashqi ask al-Qasim ibn Muhammad about the life pension and what people said about it. Al- Qasim ibn Muhammad said, ""I have only come upon people who keep to the conditions they make about their property and what they are given."" Yahya said that he heard Malik say, ""What is done in our community is that the life pension reverts to the one who makes it a life pension unless he says, 'It belongs to you and your posterity"
144501,"Malik related to me from Nafi that Abdullah ibn Umar inherited the house of Hafsa bint Umar. He said, ""Hafsa gave lodging to the daughter of Zayd ibn al-Khattab for as long as she lived. When the daughter of Zayd died, Abdullah ibn Umar took possession of the dwelling and considered that it was his"
144502,"Malik related to me from Nafi that Abdullah ibn Umar inherited the house of Hafsa bint Umar. He said, ""Hafsa gave lodging to the daughter of Zayd ibn al-Khattab for as long as she lived. When the daughter of Zayd died, Abdullah ibn Umar took possession of the dwelling and considered that it was his"
144601,Malik related to me from Rabia ibn Abi Abd ar-Rahman from Yazid
144602,Malik related to me from Rabia ibn Abi Abd ar-Rahman from Yazid
144701,Malik related to me from Ayyub ibn Musa from Muawiya ibn Abdullah ibn Badr al-Juhani that his father informed him that he stopped with a people on the way to Syria and he found a purse which had eighty dinars in it. He mentioned that to Umar ibn al-Khattab. Umar said to him
144702,Malik related to me from Ayyub ibn Musa from Muawiya ibn Abdullah ibn Badr al-Juhani that his father informed him that he stopped with a people on the way to Syria and he found a purse which had eighty dinars in it. He mentioned that to Umar ibn al-Khattab. Umar said to him
144801,Malik related to me from Nafi that a man found something and went to Abdullah ibn Umar and said to him
144802,Malik related to me from Nafi that a man found something and went to Abdullah ibn Umar and said to him
144901,"Malik narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, that he asked him about a man who rents a beast of burden and then rents it out for more than he rented it for. He said: \There is no harm in that.\"""""""""
"144902","Yahya narrated to me, from Malik, from Thurayh bin Zayd al-Dili, that he said: It has reached me that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Any house or land that was divided in the Jahiliyyah, then it is according to the division of the Jahiliyyah. And any house or land that Islam reached and it was not divided, then it is according to the division of Islam."""
145001,Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Said ibn al-Musayyab that Umar ibn al-Khattab said while he was leaning his back against the Kaba
145002,Malik related to me from Yahya ibn Said from Said ibn al-Musayyab that Umar ibn al-Khattab said while he was leaning his back against the Kaba
145101,"Malik narrated to me, from al-Ala' bin Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from his grandfather, that Uthman bin Affan gave him wealth as a qirad (a type of partnership) to work with, on the condition that the profit would be between them."
"145102","Malik narrated to me, from Hisham bin Urwah, from his father, from Yahya bin Abd al-Rahman bin Hatib, that some slaves of Hatib stole a she-camel belonging to a man from Muzaynah. They slaughtered it. This was brought to Umar bin al-Khattab, and Umar ordered Kathir bin al-Salt to cut off their hands. Then Umar said: ""I think you are starving them."" Then Umar said: ""By Allah, I will make you pay a fine that will be difficult for you."" Then he said to the Muzani: ""What was the price of your she-camel?"" The Muzani said: ""By Allah, I used to prevent it from being sold for less than four hundred dirhams."" Umar said: ""Give him eight hundred dirhams."""
145201,"Malik related to me from Said ibn Amr Shurahbil ibn Said ibn Sad ibn Ubada from his father that his father said, ''Sad ibn Ubada went out with the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, in one of his raids and his mother was dying in Madina. Someone said to her, 'Leave a testament.' She said, 'In what shall I leave a testament? The property is Sad's property.' Then she died before Sad returned. When Sad ibn Ubada returned, that was mentioned to him. Sad said, 'Messenger of Allah! Will it help her if I give sadaqa for her?' The Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said, 'Yes' Sad said, 'Such-and-such a garden is sadaqa for her,' naming the garden"
145202,"Malik related to me from Said ibn Amr Shurahbil ibn Said ibn Sad ibn Ubada from his father that his father said, ''Sad ibn Ubada went out with the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, in one of his raids and his mother was dying in Madina. Someone said to her, 'Leave a testament.' She said, 'In what shall I leave a testament? The property is Sad's property.' Then she died before Sad returned. When Sad ibn Ubada returned, that was mentioned to him. Sad said, 'Messenger of Allah! Will it help her if I give sadaqa for her?' The Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said, 'Yes' Sad said, 'Such-and-such a garden is sadaqa for her,' naming the garden"
145301,Malik related to me from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from A'isha
145302,"Malik related to me from Ibn Shihab from Urwa ibn az-Zubayr from Aisha, the wife of the Prophet, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, that she said: ""Abu Bakr as-Siddiq had given her twenty wasqs of his property from al-Ghaba as a gift. When his death approached, he said, 'By Allah, my daughter, there is no one among the people after me I would prefer to see wealthy than you, and no one I would be more distressed to see poor. I gave you twenty wasqs as a gift. If you had harvested and taken possession of it, it would have been yours. Today, however, it is the property of the heirs. Your two brothers and your two sisters are the heirs. So divide it according to the Book of Allah"
145401,Malik related to me that he heard that a man of the Ansar from the tribe of Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj
145402,Malik related to me that he heard that a man of the Ansar from the tribe of Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj
